"""Original text, factual: every number below comes from out/*.json (OSM + DEM).
Season/permit claims are deliberately omitted where OSM data doesn't state them."""
D = {
"hoverla-zaroslyak": "The short way up Ukraine's highest peak. A marked footpath leaves the Zaroslyak trailhead at about 1,290 m and climbs 740 m in 3.8 km — roughly a 20% average gradient — over Mala Hoverla (1,762 m) to the 2,061 m summit. OpenStreetMap rates the path as plain hiking (SAC T1), with no technical sections, but the steepness makes it a real mountain day: allow two to three hours up. Two springs and two camp spots are mapped within 150 m of the track.",
"pip-ivan-shybene": "A long climb from the village of Shybene, at about 850 m, to Pip Ivan Chornohirskyi (2,028 m) in the Chornohora range — 11 km and 1,180 m of ascent. The first 700 m are on a minor road, then a track and path take over; OpenStreetMap rates the trail as mountain hiking (SAC T2). Along the way a spring and camp spots are mapped near Maricheyka (1,510 m) and further up. Plan a full day.",
"petros-yasinia": "A long traverse from Lazeshchyna to Yasinia over Petros (2,020 m), 33 km with 1,340 m of ascent — a full day or an overnight. About 10 km near the start are on a minor road, then tracks and footpaths climb to the ridge; OpenStreetMap tags part of the path as alpine hiking (SAC T4). Mapped along the way: Petros, Petrosul (1,848 m), several huts and camp spots, and one seasonal spring labelled as drying up, so carry water.",
"synevyr-lopushne": "A 15 km walk from the village of Lopushne to the Synevyr lake area, climbing 600 m to a high point of 1,148 m. It mixes tracks, footpaths and minor road; OpenStreetMap carries no difficulty rating for it. Two springs and two camp spots are mapped within 150 m of the track, and Lake Synevyr (989 m) is mapped beside the track.",
"rysy-palenica": "Rysy (2,499 m) from the Palenica Białczańska parking on the Polish side of the Tatras: 12.3 km and 1,530 m of ascent. The first 7.7 km follow the wide Droga Oswalda Balzera to Morskie Oko, then a footpath climbs past Czarny Staw and the Bula pod Rysami (2,055 m) to the summit. OpenStreetMap rates the upper section demanding alpine hiking (SAC T5): expect exposure and easy scrambling. Springs and the old PTTK hut at Morskie Oko are mapped along the way.",
"odle-munkelweg": "The Adolf Munkel Weg is an easy 6 km footpath that traverses beneath the Odle / Geisler rock walls in the Dolomites, between roughly 1,860 and 2,050 m, with only 350 m of total ascent. OpenStreetMap rates it mountain hiking (SAC T2) — no technical sections are tagged. A spring (Weißbrunn / Fontana Bianca) and the Brogleshütte hut (2,047 m) are mapped on the route.",
"av1-fanes-nuvolau": "A stage of Alta Via 1, the Dolomites long-distance trail from Lago di Braies to Belluno (about 120 km). This section runs 19 km from Rifugio Fanes (2,060 m) to Rifugio Nuvolau (2,574 m) via Rifugio Averau, climbing 1,390 m in total, with a high point near Averau (2,648 m). OpenStreetMap rates parts of it demanding mountain hiking (SAC T3) and one stretch follows the Sentiero del Fronte. Lech de Lagació, Lago de Limedes and other water points are mapped near the track.",
"ramon-crater": "An 11 km section of the long trail around Makhtesh Ramon, the erosion crater in the Negev desert, from the Karne Ramon lookout to the Arod viewpoint. It follows footpaths and dirt tracks, passes Har Ramon (1,035 m) and the Borot Lots campsite, and has only 130 m of ascent. OpenStreetMap shows no drinking water within 150 m of this stretch, so carry everything you need.",
"sprengisandur-f26": "Sprengisandsleið, road F26: 154 km across Iceland's central highlands, ranging between about 250 and 830 m. Almost the whole length is gravel; OpenStreetMap tags it as four-wheel-drive only and records a ford on the route. Camp and hut sites at Nýidalur and Versalir are mapped within 150 m of the track; plan fuel and supplies before you set out. Highland F-roads are mountain tracks, not ordinary roads.",
"kjolur-f35": "Kjalvegur, road 35 (the Kjölur route): 111 km through Iceland's interior between Langjökull and Hofsjökull, from about 210 m to 674 m. OpenStreetMap tags most of it as gravel (four-wheel-drive only), with a 15 km paved section at one end. An emergency hut and two viewpoints are mapped within 150 m of the track; services are far apart, so plan fuel and supplies in advance. Conditions change quickly in the highlands, so check local road reports before you go.",
"donau-moldau-radweg": "The Donau–Moldau cycle route runs 64 km from Haidmühle, by the Czech border, down through Neureichenau, Jandelsbrunn, Breitenberg and Wegscheid to Obernzell on the Danube, descending from about 870 m to 290 m. Half of it is on quiet minor roads, with cycle paths and short stretches of path between. The route is mostly gentle, with rolling climbs of about 670 m in total. Springs, a viewpoint at Braunberg (706 m) and a youth campsite at Rannasee are mapped near the track.",
"vltava-krumlov": "Thirty-five kilometres of the Vltava from Vyšší Brod to Český Krumlov, following the river's mapped centreline downstream — it falls from about 580 m to 485 m. OpenStreetMap carries no rapids grade for this stretch so check water levels and weirs locally before you launch. Two campsites (Minikemp Rožmberk and U Nojdy) and two viewpoints are mapped within 150 m of the river.",
}
